Z||000008||bHooper||03-01-2001||04:27 PM||||i think i'm getting a line of sh!#, or getting it rubbed in my face now!  you guys gotta see this!<P><BR><I>Brian<BR>Congratulations on your upcoming Wedding! I am really happy for you!  <BR> <BR>I know you were shocked to hear from me!  I just took a deep breath before I contacted you first, I really didn't think you would write<BR>to me, that last letter I got, ouch.. a long time ago.  It is strange how you just keep popping up.  The first time in High School at a track meet, then a few years later when we were in College at a club, and again, 13 years later. </I><B>i didnt go searching her out, she found ME!... like i need this!</B><P><I>My family and I moved to San Francisco in August 2000.  It is such a great place to be, the weather is very nice, scenery is beautiful too.  My husband and I started our own Internet Security company about 3 years ago, it really took off, 12 offices in about 6 months.  <P>We sold out to Verisign, a large conglomerate two years ago, and everything has been like a whirlwind since.  This is why we bought<BR>a home in the city of San Francisco.  It's funny, I thought of you the first time I saw this home, just because I know how much you<BR>value interesting design.  (Please do not think I am trying to brag, I am not, but it is a pretty incredible home.)  The original owner, Alexander Maisin, who built and designed it was friends with Frank Lloyd Wright (OOO!  Ahhh!)  (He has actually been here!!!)  It is 11,000 square feet, has a swimming pool with a retractable roof in the home on the second level, a live Redwood tree growing up through one side!  It sits on the side of Mt. Davidson, about 1300 ft. up overlooking the Pacific.  The entire home is redwood strips.  <P>Maisin had a Russian friend who was willing to go to Japan for 6 years to learn Japanese woodworking before he assisted in the design and construction of the home. Maisin is the man who built Fisherman's Wharf in San Francisco!! He passed away about 6 years ago, sadly, I would have liked to meet him! (I still can't believe it is our home!)  The couple we purchased it from are very<BR>interesting as well.  The lady used to be married to a Hollywood Film Director and alot of the furnishings are from famous movies, or<BR>personal property acquired at auction.  I have a sofa that once belonged to Rudolph Valentino just to mention one. Hence: we bought the home completely furnished.  (With three children, I would never have the time to shop for furniture!!)  There was actually a documentary that was filmed by BBC for Japan on the house just before we bought it, it was online for awhile, if you are interested, I will see if I can locate it, or just send you some pics?! </I><P>ok, ok, lets take a vote.  Is it:<BR><B>A)</B> Bored housewife syndrom.<BR><B>B)</B> an Ex trying to rub her wonderfull exotic life in my face.<BR>or<BR><B>C)</B> the poor girl has developed an addiction to cheap wine and pain killers, producing fantasization and halucination.<P>you be the judge.<P>hoop<P><p>[This message has been edited by bHooper (edited 03-01-2001).]||||reg||1
